"artist":null,"year":null,"weight":12,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":30156349260918,"type":"artist","name":"Wayne Nelson","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":2,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":230004498270600,"type":"artist","name":"David Briggs","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":126760053580534,"type":"artist","name":"Wayne Nelson (since 1980)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":71872800944375,"type":"artist","name":"David Briggs (lead guitarist)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":56496494110188,"type":"artist","name":"Graham Goble (founding member)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":1,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"member_of":[],"genres":[{"id":251671925246370,"type":"genre","name":"Soft Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":418,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"},{"id":120453583385720,"type":"genre","name":"Prog Rock","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":114,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_genres":[{"id":136113600818764,"type":"genre","name":"Yacht Rock","n":3}],"labels":[{"id":181795954102141,"type":"label","name":"Record label (unnamed)","artist":null,"year":null,"weight":8,"yt":null,"src":"ensayos"}],"album_labels":[],"own_scenes":[],"scenes":[],"book_tracks":[],"mentions":[],"podcast":[{"kind":"podcast","title":"Preference for The Beatles Comparison (1977)","body":"In a 1977 interview with Cameron Crowe, lead guitarist David Briggs specifically requested that the band not be compared to Crosby, Stills, Nash & Young solely for their harmonies. Crowe himself felt their musical spirit was closer to The Beatles, a comparison the band was \"completely fine with,\" suggesting an appreciation for a more experimental approach. \"when the interview was over David Briggs the lead guitarist ask crow to not compare them to crossbeast Hills nation young just because they use harmonies and crow said that he was not going to do that he said he thought that their musical spirit. Was a lot closer to the Beatles which I take to mean a bit more experimental.\"","year":1977,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-51-the-softer-side-of-70s-rock"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Criticism as \"Too Bland\" or \"Too Soft\"","body":"The band frequently faced criticism that their music was \"too bland, too safe, too soft,\" highlighting a common perception leveled against softer rock acts. \"Little river band was told that their music was too bland to save to soft.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-51-the-softer-side-of-70s-rock"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Distinct US vs. Australian Album Releases","body":"A notable characteristic of Australian bands, including Little River Band, was the practice of issuing different record versions in Australia compared to the United States. For instance, the Australian pressing of *Sleeper Catcher* featured a \"much more prog rock type of opening\" that was \"creative and complex,\" contrasting with the US version. \"for Australian bands for example there was often a different record issued in Australia than one that was released in the United States.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-51-the-softer-side-of-70s-rock"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Esteem from John Lennon and Frank Sinatra for \"Reminiscing\"","body":"The song \"Reminiscing\" was a particular favorite of John Lennon among 1970s songs, and he was known to listen to it \"on repeat.\" Frank Sinatra also expressed his liking for the song. \"this was one of John Lennon's favorites maybe he loved the harmonies as much as I do. It was one of his favorite 70 songs and he would just listen to it on repeat Frank Sinatra liked it too.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-51-the-softer-side-of-70s-rock"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Recognized Musicianship and Commerciality","body":"Despite criticisms of being \"soft,\" the band members were acknowledged as \"accomplished musicians, writers and arrangers.\" Cameron Crowe, in a 1977 article, described them as having \"melodic lead guitarists and for sophisticated singer songwriters\" who had \"mastered an effortless commerciality.\" \"these were accomplished musicians writers and arrangers Cameron Crow wrote an article about little river band in 1977 and he described it like this with melodic lead guitarists and for sophisticated singer songwriters little river band has mastered an effortless commerciality.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-51-the-softer-side-of-70s-rock"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Significant Lineup Changes and Nationality Shift","body":"Little River Band has undergone numerous lineup changes, so extensive that tracking them would require a flowchart. The band, originally Australian, eventually became an American band with \"no original members,\" though Wayne Nelson has been a member since approximately 1980. \"it would take a flow chart and perhaps a legal textbook to track all the changes and the bands lineup and dig into the how and why what used to be an Australian band is now an American band with no original members although way Nelson has been with them since about 1980.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 51 - The Softer Side of 70s Rock","url":"https://seventies.libsyn.com/ep-51-the-softer-side-of-70s-rock"}]}],"awards":[],"concerts":[],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":7494004907167,"type":"album","name":"After Hours","list_id":254136942605146,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Yacht Rock","src":"musthear","rank":120,"year":1976,"note":null,"yt":"-MSGtz8wK_4","scaruffi":null},{"id":5635959393423,"type":"album","name":"Diamantina Cocktail","list_id":254136942605146,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Yacht Rock","src":"musthear","rank":53,"year":1977,"note":null,"yt":"u5b9h2sIGIw","scaruffi":null},{"id":116492426557517,"type":"song","name":"Reminiscing","work_year":1978,"list_id":49580741702968,"list":"Billboard Year-End 1978","src":"singles","rank":65,"year":1978,"note":"","yt":"XBETVhHpcPk","scaruffi":null},{"id":116492426557517,"type":"song","name":"Reminiscing","work_year":1978,"list_id":132335824845498,"list":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","src":"charts","rank":65,"year":1978,"note":null,"yt":"XBETVhHpcPk","scaruffi":null},{"id":234515851218861,"type":"song","name":"Lady","work_year":1979,"list_id":272107381899114,"list":"Billboard Year-End 1979","src":"singles","rank":42,"year":1979,"note":"","yt":"uMV6-dacuuc","scaruffi":null},{"id":251644209490498,"type":"song","name":"Lonesome Loser","work_year":1979,"list_id":272107381899114,"list":"Billboard Year-End 1979","src":"singles","rank":66,"year":1979,"note":"","yt":"8cqhZ47Hg7g","scaruffi":null},{"id":234515851218861,"type":"song","name":"Lady","work_year":1979,"list_id":132335824845498,"list":"Billboard Year-End Hot 100","src":"charts","rank":42,"ye